[1;36m THE RISKS OF AN UNENCRYPTED BBS
[0;36m and what it means for your privacy
[0;37m 
[0;37m Four short pages. They take a minute,
[0;37m and they are the minute worth spending.
[0;37m 
[1;33m TELNET IS NOT ENCRYPTED
[0;37m 
[0;37m Telnet has no encryption. It never has,
[0;37m and on this board it never will.
[0;37m 
[0;37m Everything you type crosses the network
[0;37m as readable text. What you say, and the
[0;37m password you type to get in.
[0;37m 
[0;37m That is not an oversight. It is the
[0;37m price of letting a 1982 computer call,
[0;37m and a C64 cannot do encryption. Better
[0;37m to tell you than quietly pretend.
[0;37m 
[1;30m Page 1 of 4
[1;33m WHAT THAT ACTUALLY RISKS
[0;37m 
[0;37m Reading your password needs two things:
[0;37m a sniffer, which is any program that
[0;37m records network traffic, and a position
[0;37m on the path between you and this board.
[0;37m 
[0;37m Who has that? Whoever runs the wifi you
[0;37m are on. Whoever runs the office
[0;37m network. Your internet provider, and
[0;37m the board's. Somebody who has put
[0;37m themselves in the middle on purpose.
[0;37m 
[0;37m Not a stranger on the internet, then.
[0;37m It takes access, and most people simply
[0;37m do not have it.
[0;37m 
[0;37m Low risk. Not no risk. Worth one unique
[0;37m password, not an afternoon of worry.
[0;37m 
[1;30m Page 2 of 4
[1;33m YOUR PASSWORD ON THIS BOARD
[0;37m 
[0;37m It is never stored as you typed it. It
[0;37m is salted and hashed with SHA-256, a
[0;37m thousand rounds, and only the result is
[0;37m written down. Nobody can read it back,
[0;37m including the sysop.
[0;37m 
[0;37m That protects the file if the file is
[0;37m stolen. It does nothing for the wire,
[0;37m where you typed it in the clear.
[0;37m 
[0;37m And a hash is not magic. A common
[0;37m password still falls to a lookup table.
[0;37m 
[1;30m Page 3 of 4
[1;33m WHAT THIS BOARD KNOWS
[0;37m 
[0;37m The sysop sees your handle, the address
[0;37m you called from, when you called and for
[0;37m how long, and the last command you ran.
[0;37m Staff can watch a node. Messages you
[0;37m leave sit in a file until they are read.
[0;37m 
[0;37m Assume whoever owns the machine can read
[0;37m what is on it. That is true everywhere.
[0;37m Here you at least know who they are.
[0;37m 
[0;37m Honestly? It is a hobby board on a five
[0;37m dollar chip, and it is conversation.
[0;37m 
[1;32m Use a password you use nowhere else.
[0;37m 
